<!--
    Guards the byte-aware gate on the single-file Parquet parallel-read path
    (`StorageFile` + `computeBucketsByCountAndBytes`).

    A single local Parquet file with enough row groups is fanned out into several
    per-row-group "bucket" sources so a big scan uses many cores instead of one.
    But fanning out a *light* query — one that reads only a few small columns — is
    pure loss: each extra source pays open / reader-init / pipeline-port overhead
    with almost no data to amortize it over, which visibly slows sub-second queries.

    The gate fixes that: it only splits when the columns the query reads add up to
    at least `input_format_parquet_min_bytes_to_split` compressed bytes, and then
    picks the bucket count from `input_format_parquet_bytes_per_split_bucket`.

    The file below has one big String column and two tiny UInt8 columns, split into
    ~160 row groups (so the pre-gate row-group-count logic would fan even a
    one-tiny-column read out into several sources). With the thresholds set here:
      * `sum(k1)` reads a few MB  -> below the split threshold  -> single source (fast),
      * `sum(length(big))` reads ~100+ MB -> above it           -> split across sources.
    If a change ever makes the narrow query split again, the first query regresses.
-->
<test>
    <settings>
        <max_threads>8</max_threads>
        <input_format_parquet_min_bytes_to_split>33554432</input_format_parquet_min_bytes_to_split>
        <input_format_parquet_bytes_per_split_bucket>8388608</input_format_parquet_bytes_per_split_bucket>
    </settings>

    <fill_query><![CDATA[
        INSERT INTO FUNCTION file('test_parquet_bucket_split.parquet', Parquet)
        SELECT * FROM generateRandom('big String, k1 UInt8, k2 UInt8', 1, 64, 1)
        LIMIT 4000000
        SETTINGS output_format_parquet_row_group_size = 25000, engine_file_truncate_on_insert = 1
    ]]></fill_query>

    <!-- Narrow read: touches one tiny column, stays below the split threshold, so
         the gate must keep it a single source. Regresses if it is fanned out. -->
    <query><![CDATA[SELECT sum(k1) FROM file('test_parquet_bucket_split.parquet', Parquet) FORMAT Null]]></query>

    <!-- Same shape, still narrow (two tiny columns). -->
    <query><![CDATA[SELECT sum(k1), sum(k2) FROM file('test_parquet_bucket_split.parquet', Parquet) FORMAT Null]]></query>

    <!-- Heavy read: the big String column is well above the split threshold, so it
         must be parallelised across bucket sources — the case the feature exists for. -->
    <query><![CDATA[SELECT sum(length(big)) FROM file('test_parquet_bucket_split.parquet', Parquet) FORMAT Null]]></query>
</test>
